2186242242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243813 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d6fa166b-2a28-5db4-b15a-93030703eeb9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242866 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243835 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e592fca1-0175-5fcf-92d8-6643cf8b3eff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241673 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242516 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243867 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5fa1c74-bebe-573e-99bc-a11d6c3f095e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243813 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+y}{3-2x-y}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0db4ae1-7ca8-5d44-90b5-79a2a42ad1ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244105 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2x+y}{3-(2x+y)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2+\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-2 & =\\frac{z}{3-z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{6-z}{3-z}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{3-z}{6-z})\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int (\\frac{z-3}{z-6})\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\int (\\frac{3}{z-6}+1)\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|z-6|+z & =x+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6|+2x+y & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+y-6| & =-\\frac{x+y}{3}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{K}{3}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+y-6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108735" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3x+6y.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+2y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "05430704-5a00-5ff9-8973-3229343f5e50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244156 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3(x+2y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+2y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+2\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =3z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=6z+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=6z+1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{8x-12y+1}{12x-18y+3}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=2x-3y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "463a8e36-eaf0-59f2-9877-1d9a9eb456ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244159 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4(2x-3y)+1}{6(2x-3y)+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-3y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-3\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(2-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}) & =\\frac{4z+1}{6z+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{1}{2z+1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108750" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2x+8y-1}{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x+4y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "458fe83e-5ab5-56bf-a1ff-1862be912e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244160 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2(x+4y)-1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+4y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+4\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1) & =\\frac{2z-1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=2z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2+y-x}{x-y}.\n$$\n\nUsing a substitution of the form $z=x-y,$ transform the differential equation into an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z).$ What is the function $f(z)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52822c39-606a-5a54-9e92-3a8ac02903e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244161 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-(x-y)}{x-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2-z}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(z)=\\frac{2(z-1)}{z}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243544 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108780" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3x+3y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(g(x,y))=-6x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0b7b4d1c-df4d-5986-bef2-30931183e55d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244164 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3(x+y))-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2{sin}^{2}⁡(3z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int {csc}^{2}⁡(3z)\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡(3z) & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x+3y)=-6x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=3x+3y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+5y)-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|g(x,y)|=10x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e58752d6-0044-55ba-a578-6076003cbc1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244172 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5(x+y))-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5z).\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2 \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cot}⁡(5z)\\,\\text{d}z & =2\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =2x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5z)| & =10x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y)|=10x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+5y).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272669 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{4z+5}{z+1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186272669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1fd4f187-0154-57a7-9de9-e48447827a62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273214 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2z+1}{z+1}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186273214 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921860