3 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488136 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc2debc3-d327-56e2-a61e-77f8bfd4b277" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487861 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186487680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488172 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8cb194d-0fce-5c7b-940d-dfeca915097d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1069 "q-149760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2+4{x}^{2}-4xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\ng(x,y)=x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19e48035-430d-58ef-92e6-a92cd0b0c10e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488190 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2+(2x-y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=2x-y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =2+{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}={z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{z} & =x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2x-y}=x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2x-y}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186488172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}+8xy+4{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation can be expressed in the form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(g(x,y))=2x+C,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant. What is $g(x,y)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6beef5a1-8cca-5685-897f-0ef5f4dc810f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186488222 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we factor the right-hand side of the given equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =4(x+y)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz=x+y.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=1+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-1 & =4{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing this equation in the form ${z}^{′}(x)=f(z)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=4{z}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is separable. So, we separate the variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{4{z}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2z) & =x+K \\\\ \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2z) & =2x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2K.$\n\nFinally, we write the equation in terms of $y$ and $x$ using our original substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+2y)=2x+C\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ng(x,y)=2x+2y.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
